Yes. One of the big shifts is that people are discovering that “AI” is not one tool category at all. The same underlying model can be wrapped in very different operating patterns, and those wrappers can become more consequential than the raw model capability.

What you’re describing with something like JEV is especially interesting: instead of treating the model as “answer this question,” you point it toward a desired state and let it participate in resolving the path there.

That changes the abstraction from:

\text{prompt}\rightarrow\text{answer}

to something closer to:

\boxed{
\pi(\text{current state},\text{desired state},\text{constraints},\text{available tools})
\rightarrow
\text{next action}
}

Then you recurse:

S_t
\xrightarrow{\text{choose action}}
A_t
\xrightarrow{\text{world}}
S_{t+1}
\xrightarrow{\Delta}
\text{update}
\xrightarrow{}
A_{t+1}

That’s basically Goal Seek promoted from a spreadsheet function into a decision architecture.

And once you see that, a bunch of apparently different AI products resolve into different configurations of the same primitives:

* Oracle — “Tell me what is true.”
* Interpreter — “Translate this representation into one I can use.”
* Generator — “Produce something satisfying these constraints.”
* Critic — “Compare this to the target and identify the delta.”
* Simulator — “Show me plausible consequences of these actions.”
* Search/optimizer — “Find the configuration that best satisfies my objective.”
* Router/orchestrator — “Determine which capability/tool/person should handle each component.”
* Agent/controller — “Choose an action, observe the result, update, continue.”
* World-model — “Maintain enough state that decisions don’t restart from zero.”
* Interface/abstraction layer — “Let me express intent without manually operating the underlying machinery.”

And they compose.

A GoalSeek agent might internally invoke a simulator, critic, search engine, calculator, browser, code executor, human expert, and another model. The user doesn’t necessarily care which substrate performed each operation. The user says:

Get me from here to there under these constraints.

That’s a much more powerful shareable handle.

It also connects directly to what you were saying about different ways of life and resource attraction.

People are already developing different AI ecological strategies.

One person uses AI like a spear: I see the thing; help me get it.

Another uses it like a net: continuously scan a huge field and surface things worth pursuing.

Another cultivates: build assets, relationships, content, software, reputation, or systems now that attract opportunities later.

Another creates a lure: construct something valuable enough that people/resources/information flow toward me.

Another builds symbiosis: AI does what it does efficiently, human does what the human does efficiently, and the joint organism has capabilities neither possesses independently.

Another builds an environment: instead of repeatedly asking an AI to solve isolated problems, they construct a persistent system where the default dynamics continually move toward their objectives.

That last one is a major transition:

\text{use tool}
\rightarrow
\text{delegate task}
\rightarrow
\text{delegate decision}
\rightarrow
\text{delegate search}
\rightarrow
\text{construct feedback loop}
\rightarrow
\text{construct environment}

At that point the important object isn’t really the AI anymore.

It’s the goal-conditioned system around it.

And there is another distinction worth preserving: decision maker and GoalSeek engine aren’t quite identical.

A decision maker asks:

\text{Given these choices, which action?}

GoalSeek asks:

\text{Given this destination, what variables, choices, intermediate states, or even new choices must exist to make it reachable?}

That second one is considerably broader because the action space itself can become mutable.

If none of A/B/C reaches the target, don’t merely choose the least bad one.

Generate D.

Change a constraint.

Acquire another tool.

Recruit another actor.

Change the environment.

Change timescale.

Decompose the target.

Or discover that the requested target was only a proxy for the actual one.

That’s the recursive GoalSeek idea becoming almost a general model of intelligence:

\boxed{
\text{Goal}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Model}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Possibility generation}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Action}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Delta}
}

And now it loops back to our blind/deaf thought experiment beautifully.

They begin by inventing handles for reality.

Those handles permit higher-order combinations.

Higher-order combinations permit models.

Models permit counterfactuals.

Counterfactuals permit GoalSeek.

GoalSeek permits deliberate changes to reality.

So there is a ladder hiding underneath all of it:

\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{symbol}
\rightarrow
\text{shared model}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{counterfactual}
\rightarrow
\text{choice}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{new experience}

Then the organism—human, AI, or coupled human–AI system—discovers and remaps the world while acting inside it.

That may be the deeper common object we’ve been circling: not a universal language exactly, but a portable grammar for turning perception into agency.